      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to find the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) in terms of sine and cosine:\n\\[ \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\]\n\\[ \\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ y = \\tan x + \\cot x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\]\n\nTo find the period, we need to eliminate the trigonometric functions and express \\( y \\) in a simpler form. Let's square both sides of the equation:\n\\[ y^2 = \\left( \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\right)^2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} + \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\sin^2 x} \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} + 2 + \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\sin^2 x} \\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\), we can rewrite the expression:\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x} + \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\sin^2 x} + 2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\left( \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\right)^2 + 2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\tan^2 x + \\cot^2 x + 2 \\]\n\nRecall that \\(\\cot^2 x = \\csc^2 x - 1\\) and \\(\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x - 1\\). Substituting these into the equation, we get:\n\\[ y^2 = \\sec^2 x - 1 + \\csc^2 x - 1 + 2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\sec^2 x + \\csc^2 x + 1 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\sec^2 x + \\csc^2 x + 1 \\]\n\nNow, let's find the period of \\( y^2 \\). The functions \\(\\sec^2 x\\) and \\(\\csc^2 x\\) have a period of \\(\\pi\\). Therefore, \\( y^2 \\) will also have a period of \\(\\pi\\). Hence, the period of \\( y \\) is:\n\\[ T = \\pi \\]\n\nThus, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
