      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to analyze the periods of the individual trigonometric functions \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\).\n\nFirst, recall the periods of the basic trigonometric functions:\n- The period of \\(\\tan x\\) is \\( \\pi \\).\n- The period of \\(\\cot x\\) is also \\( \\pi \\).\n\nNext, we consider the sum \\(\\tan x + \\cot x\\). We need to find a value \\( T \\) such that:\n\\[ y(x + T) = y(x) \\]\nfor all \\( x \\).\n\nLet's compute \\( y(x + T) \\):\n\\[ y(x + T) = \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) \\]\n\nUsing the periodicity properties of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\):\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) = \\tan x + k_\\tan T \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot(x + T) = \\cot x + k_\\cot T \\]\nwhere \\( k_\\tan T \\) and \\( k_\\cot T \\) are integers.\n\nFor \\( y(x + T) \\) to be equal to \\( y(x) \\), we need:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) = \\tan x + \\cot x \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ \\tan x + k_\\tan T + \\cot x + k_\\cot T = \\tan x + \\cot x \\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\), we get:\n\\[ k_\\tan T = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad k_\\cot T = 0 \\]\n\nThus, \\( T \\) must be a common multiple of the periods of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\). Since both have a period of \\( \\pi \\), the smallest such \\( T \\) is:\n\\[ T = \\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
